## What you can resell

Each service can be sold on its own or bundled into a monthly retainer you price yourself.

| Service | What your client receives | Turnaround |
|---|---|---|
| Technical SEO audit | Written report, prioritised fix list and a developer-ready spreadsheet in your branding | 5 working days after access for sites up to around 500 pages |
| Local SEO | Google Business Profile work, location pages, citation clean-up and review strategy | Agreed per project |
| Content briefs | A brief per page: the questions to answer, the entities to cover and the structure to follow | Agreed per project |
| Topical map | A structured plan of pillar topics and supporting pages | Agreed per project |
| Technical fixes | Specified fixes for your developers, or changes made directly where access allows | Agreed per project |
| AI visibility check | What ChatGPT, Copilot, Google AI Overviews and Perplexity say about the client for its main queries | Agreed per project |
| Monthly SEO retainer | An agreed monthly scope with a plain-English report your account managers can present | Monthly |

The audit scope matches my direct [technical SEO audit](/services/technical-seo-audit/). AI answers vary from one run to the next. An AI visibility check reports what I observed on the day. It is a baseline and makes no promise of inclusion.

## White label SEO or SEO reseller: what is the difference?

The two terms describe the same arrangement from different sides. **White label SEO** describes the work: it carries your brand only. **SEO reseller** describes your role: you buy the work from a supplier and sell it to your client at your own price.

In practice you get both. You resell SEO and every deliverable is white labelled. Some providers use "reseller programme" for a fixed menu of packages. I work from an agreed scope per client instead. The work then fits the site.

## Who is white label SEO for?

White label SEO suits agencies that already own client relationships but lack senior search skills in-house. Four types of agency use it most.

**Web design agencies** often build good sites that then do not rank. Clients then ask why traffic has not arrived. A white label partner lets you answer with a deliverable instead of a referral. You also keep the recurring work.

**PPC and paid media agencies** see the search data every day but have no one to act on organic. Reselling SEO lets you offer the organic side without hiring. Paid and organic plans stay in one place.

**Social, creative and marketing agencies** run brand and campaign work well but treat organic search as an afterthought. You can add a [technical SEO audit](/services/technical-seo-audit/), a topical map and ongoing optimisation to existing retainers.

**Full-service agencies without an in-house SEO** face the biggest gap. A retained client asks for search and you either turn the work down or sub-contract in a hurry. A standing partner means search is always available to the same standard.

## Local SEO reseller services

Local SEO is the service agencies are asked for most by small and regional businesses. I deliver it under your brand for clients that serve a town, a region or several branches.

A local SEO resale typically covers:

1. **Google Business Profile:** categories, services, descriptions, photos and a posting plan.
2. **Location pages:** one useful page per area served, without thin duplicated copy.
3. **Citations:** consistent name, address and phone details across the directories that matter.
4. **Reviews:** a simple process for asking customers for reviews and replying to them.
5. **Local reporting:** calls, direction requests and enquiries tracked where the data allows.

Scope is set per client because a single shop and a ten-branch business need different work. My direct [local SEO](/services/local-seo/) page shows how I run it for my own clients.

## What does each deliverable include?

The white label service covers the full SEO delivery stack. The scope is the same work I deliver to direct clients.

**Technical SEO audits** identify crawl, indexation, speed and architecture issues. They come back as a prioritised fix list your developers can action.

**Semantic SEO strategy and topical maps** define what a client site must cover in its niche. The map turns a business into a structured plan of pillar topics, supporting pages and internal links.

**On-page optimisation** restructures existing pages around search intent, covering headings, internal links and content depth through on-page SEO work.

**Content briefs** give your writers or your client's writers a specification for every page. The [content briefs](/services/content-briefs/) list the entities to cover, the questions to answer and the structure to follow.

**Keyword and demand research** maps real search behaviour to commercial intent. The plan then targets queries that lead to enquiries.

**AI visibility work** checks how AI answers describe a client and which sources they cite. The fixes are the same ones that help in search: clear facts, consistent entity details and pages that answer the question.

**Monthly reports** come in your template. Each one explains progress in plain language your account managers can present without translating SEO jargon.

## How does the partnership work?

The partnership keeps your agency in front of the client at all times.

1. **Partner call.** You talk me through one client site and the work you want to sell.
2. **Agreed scope.** Each client gets a written scope: deliverables, timings and your price to me.
3. **Access and brief.** You arrange access to the client's site, Search Console and analytics, and share any background.
4. **Delivery.** I do the work and send it to you unbranded or in your template.
5. **Review.** You present it. I can join a call as a member of your team if you arrange it.

**You get direct senior access.** The person you brief is the person who does the analysis. No junior account handler sits in between.

**Capacity is limited.** As a sole practitioner I take on a small number of partnerships at once. That keeps the work senior. Plan around availability rather than assuming unlimited throughput.

## How is reseller pricing set?

Partner pricing is agreed per project from the scope for each client site. There is no fixed trade price list.

My direct prices to my own clients are published on my own pages. Two examples are the [£495 technical SEO audit](/services/technical-seo-audit/) and local SEO from £600 per month. They show what an end client would pay me directly. You can set your own margin from there.

Two models cover most arrangements:

- **Per-client monthly scope:** a fixed monthly scope per client site that you wrap inside your own retainer at your chosen price.
- **Per-deliverable projects:** individual audits, topical maps or brief batches ordered as projects demand. There is no ongoing commitment.

Your client-facing rate is yours to set.

## How do I protect your client relationships?

The agency owns the relationship, the contract and the invoice. That rule has no exceptions.

I do not contact, market to or solicit your clients, during or after an engagement. I pass any client who contacts me directly back to you. Work I produce for your clients is delivered to your agency and is yours to present, keep and reuse.

Deliverables carry no trace of my involvement unless you choose otherwise. I'm happy to work under an NDA. Confidentiality, non-solicitation and ownership of deliverables can go in writing before work starts.

